 (snapshot capability established)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"With no LVM package or repo access available, the executor built a functionally equivalent block-level snapshot mechanism using device-mapper: a snapshot-origin target over a loopback-backed image formatted xfs and mounted at /srv/data, then a persistent dm 'snapshot' target (data-snap) capturing baseline state. dmsetup status confirmed an active snapshot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e7bec70e3bf54eff\",\"cmd-3b027ce3545a43a1\",\"cmd-cd75d2e9ae4f4226\"]},{\"id\":\"directory-modified\",\"requirement\":\"Directory is modified after the snapshot is taken\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"records.csv deleted, settings.conf overwritten with corrupted content, and a stray oops.txt added; checksums directly show divergence from the pre-modification baseline.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a74c4c25e5034f03\"]},{\"id\":\"exact-restore\",\"requirement\":\"Restore /srv/data to the exact content captured by the earlier snapshot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An initial rsync-based restore attempt silently failed (rsync not installed) and left the modified state in place, but the executor detected this and performed a corrective restore (removing files absent from the snapshot, cp -a of snapshot content) and verified via sha256 manifest diff that restored content was byte-identical ('IDENTICAL') to the original baseline.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d66dc3752a054968\",\"cmd-5990cea1dadc4798\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart state (boot_id d7a80301..., mount, checksums) was captured, shutdown -r now was issued and acknowledged (connection closed), the node was polled until reachable, and post-restart state showed a different boot_id (3cc9d27e...) matching the fixed after_executor lifecycle snapshot, with srv-data.service active, /srv/data auto-remounted, and checksums matching the original baseline exactly. A fresh dm-snapshot was also re-created post-restart to keep ongoing recoverability.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-10eda553855f4013\",\"cmd-065d511b7f694795\",\"cmd-05b751b883fa46fa\",\"cmd-f284fb5d0a624d07\",\"cmd-6db0f2ca03814e88\",\"cmd-8bf2e3a2b78f4d19\",\"cmd-cc4656c7663741dc\",\"cmd-4ed6a7d5e48f45cd\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.88,\"summary\":\"Commands were largely discovery, construction, or verification with clear causal roles. Two yum-install attempts (cmd-d46d1706c9b0417d, cmd-791183ff137047c7) failed due to lack of root/repos and made no state change — legitimate discovery of constraints, not unwanted mutations. The rsync-based restore attempt inside cmd-d66dc3752a054968 failed silently (binary missing) and left the corrupted state briefly in place, but this was immediately corrected by cmd-5990cea1dadc4798 with no lasting residue. The snapshot mount at /mnt/data-snap was cleanly unmounted afterward (cmd-f8ad2cc7964f486b). Minor low-risk residue remains in /tmp (baseline_manifest.sha256, restored_manifest.sha256, reboot.log) per the after_executor temporary-files diff — these are small evidence artifacts, not destructive or security-weakening, and reasonably tied to verification. No failed services, no unnecessary package/security changes, and the persistent systemd unit and dm devices are all purposeful and retained as intended by the task.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d46d1706c9b0417d\",\"cmd-791183ff137047c7\",\"cmd-d66dc3752a054968\",\"cmd-5990cea1dadc4798\",\"cmd-f8ad2cc7964f486b\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct evidence: a real snapshot mechanism (dm-snapshot, since LVM was unavailable) was built for /srv/data, the directory was modified post-snapshot, and it was restored to be byte-identical to the earlier snapshot (confirmed via sha256 manifest diff). The independent node-restart evidence protocol is also fully satisfied — pre-restart baseline, acknowledged restart, a changed boot identity cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and confirmed post-restart recovery of the service, mount, and data. Operational hygiene is good: the only friction was a failed rsync-based restore step that was immediately corrected, plus minor evidence-file residue in /tmp.\",\"limitations\":[\"The initial restore attempt via rsync failed silently due to a missing binary; the trace shows the subsequent corrective command fully achieved exact restoration, but relies on chronological ordering of two adjacent commands rather than a single atomic step.\",\"Package-health checks were unavailable (timed out) in both before_prepare and after_executor snapshots, so package/RPM database integrity could not be independently cross-verified.\",\"The snapshot solution depends on manually managed loopback devices and a custom systemd unit rather than native LVM; this is a reasonable substitute given no repo access, but represents a different persistence surface than a standard LVM-based snapshot rollback.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"snapshot-mechanism","requirement":"Keep /srv/data recoverable through filesystem snapshots (snapshot capability established)","status":"satisfied","summary":"With no LVM package or repo access available, the executor built a functionally equivalent block-level snapshot mechanism using device-mapper: a snapshot-origin target over a loopback-backed image formatted xfs and mounted at /srv/data, then a persistent dm 'snapshot' target (data-snap) capturing baseline state. dmsetup status confirmed an active snapshot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e7bec70e3bf54eff","cmd-3b027ce3545a43a1","cmd-cd75d2e9ae4f4226"]},{"id":"d